13. DATABASE DESIGN

Use a relational database, preferably PostgreSQL.

Design normalized schemas with migrations and appropriate foreign keys, unique constraints, indexes, and deletion rules.

Include entities such as:

* users
* roles
* permissions
* user_role_assignments
* customers
* leads
* lead_activities
* appointments
* services
* portfolio_projects
* portfolio_images
* projects
* project_members
* project_tasks
* project_milestones
* quotations
* quotation_versions
* quotation_items
* quotation_approvals
* payments
* expenses
* documents
* notifications
* audit_logs
* company_settings

Add join tables and supporting tables where required.

Use explicit created_at and updated_at fields, appropriate timestamps, consistent timezone handling, and indexes for common search and reporting queries.

Use soft deletion only where appropriate. Financial records and issued quotations must preserve necessary auditability.

Use database transactions for operations that modify related records.

Create seed data for development only. Clearly distinguish demo records from real business records.

14. TECHNICAL ARCHITECTURE

First inspect the existing repository, runtime, installed dependencies, available services, and deployment environment.

Preserve working code and existing project conventions wherever practical. Do not overwrite a working project blindly.

If starting from scratch, use a maintainable TypeScript architecture.

Suggested stack:

* Next.js and React for the web application.
* TypeScript with strict checking.
* Tailwind CSS and reusable accessible UI components.
* PostgreSQL for relational data.
* A suitable migration and database-access layer, such as Prisma or Drizzle.
* A secure authentication solution appropriate to the hosting environment.
* A server-side application layer with validated API endpoints.
* Object storage for images and documents.
* Automated tests and CI checks.

Use either well-organized Next.js server functionality or a separate NestJS backend when justified by deployment or business complexity. Do not create unnecessary duplicate backend services.

Keep business logic separate from UI components.

Validate all API inputs using a schema-validation library such as Zod.

Use environment variables or a supported secret manager for credentials.

Provide a complete .env.example containing placeholder names only, never real secrets.

Include database migrations, reproducible setup instructions, development commands, production build commands, and deployment documentation.

Use a background worker and queue only when required for reliable asynchronous processing.

