19. IMPLEMENTATION WORKFLOW

Follow this execution sequence.

PHASE 1 — Repository Inspection and Requirements

* Inspect the existing codebase and runtime.
* Identify existing functionality, limitations, and reusable components.
* Confirm the implementation plan and identify missing credentials or business assets.
* Record the architecture decisions.

PHASE 2 — Design System and Responsive Public Website

* Establish the design tokens and reusable components.
* Implement navigation, footer, home page, services, portfolio, project details, about, contact, and quotation form.
* Verify Arabic RTL and mobile layouts.

PHASE 3 — Database and Authentication

* Implement schemas and migrations.
* Create authentication and server-enforced authorization.
* Seed development-only data.
* Verify protected routes and API access.

PHASE 4 — CRM and Customer Operations

* Implement leads, customers, assignments, activity history, follow-ups, and appointments.
* Connect public lead submissions to the CRM.

PHASE 5 — Project Management

* Implement projects, team assignments, tasks, milestones, deadlines, and project documents.

PHASE 6 — Quotations and Financial Management

* Implement quotation versions, item calculations, approval workflow, Arabic PDF output, expenses, payments, and reporting.

PHASE 7 — Administration and Content Management

* Complete portfolio, service, company settings, user management, dashboard metrics, notifications, and audit logs.

PHASE 8 — Integration and Hardening

* Test full workflows.
* Fix security, data consistency, responsive UI, and performance issues.
* Verify permissions and error handling.

PHASE 9 — Production Preparation

* Run migrations in the intended environment only after reviewing them.
* Configure production settings and monitoring.
* Document deployment and recovery.
* Run production smoke tests when deployment access is available.

20. EXECUTION RULES

* Start by inspecting the available repository and tools.
* Do not merely generate a lengthy specification and stop.
* Implement real functionality incrementally.
* Reuse existing infrastructure when it is appropriate.
* Do not replace working components unnecessarily.
* Do not fabricate APIs, credentials, database connections, test results, or deployment status.
* Do not leave important buttons or forms as decorative placeholders.
* Clearly label any functionality that depends on unavailable third-party credentials.
* Use realistic development fixtures, never fake production data.
* Keep the application maintainable and modular.
* Prefer working end-to-end workflows over numerous incomplete features.
* Maintain a checklist of completed, pending, blocked, and tested requirements.
* If context or execution limits prevent completion in one run, finish the current safe task, preserve the work, document the exact remaining steps, and continue from the existing codebase rather than restarting.
* Ask questions only when a missing decision genuinely blocks implementation. Otherwise use the documented defaults and keep configurable values editable.

